# From SŪTRADHĀRA — D-12 part 3 rules on an asset that is not one of the rows it governs

M0-T39 executed the telemetry repair (the one I dropped) and stopped short of the orphan rows with
a finding I would not have caught. **Verified in code by the KĀRAKA, and I am relaying rather than
re-deriving — marked as such.**

## The conflation

D-12 part 3 declines re-dispatch for the orphaned `asset_throughput` rows on the grounds that
**"ka_gochara_sweep's `@register` is genuinely removed."**

**`ka_gochara_sweep` is not one of the three orphans.**

The three are `ka_kota_chakra`, `ka_vedha_gochara` and `mi_sankalpa` — and the KĀRAKA verified in
source that **all three carry a live `@register`**: `services/ka_kota_chakra/writer.py:193`,
`services/ka_vedha_gochara/writer.py:250`, `pipeline/orchestrator/writers/mi_sankalpa.py:76`.

So part 3's premise is true of a different asset, and part 3 resolves into part 4, which rules only
on the `ka_gochara_sweep` rows. **No ruling reaches the three orphans at all.**

## Why that leaves the KĀRAKA with no legal move, which is why it stopped

- a died-state is **H6** — the runs did not die; each has a `build_run_assets` row reading
  `complete` with `ended_at` bit-identical to `last_built_at`. The run finished; the promotion did
  not.
- `lit` is **H4** — a completed-run record is a proxy, not the promotion predicate's verdict.
- `stale` needs a **G3** ruling nobody has made.

It left them untouched and dispatched no build. That is the correct outcome of an incorrect
premise, and it is worth noticing that the invariant held even though the ruling had drifted.

**The practical consequence is that re-dispatch may be available after all.** D-12 part 3 ruled it
impossible because the writer was gone. For these three the writer is present. Whether that changes
your ruling is yours; I have queued nothing and dispatched no build.

## What did execute, and what it refused to widen into

**T-5 only: 93 rows of `estimated_seconds` from measured medians.** `NULL` went 119 → 35, and the
35 assets with no completed run **stay NULL** — nothing invented (D-6 condition 4 / H6). Zero cells
in any other column changed, across 5,120 compared.

Every D-6 condition-3 count was re-measured immediately before the write and **matched the proposal
exactly**, so nothing returned to you on that ground.

Two things about its method are worth your ledger:

- **It made the restore drill discriminating rather than trivially passing.** Inside one
  always-rolled-back transaction it applied the *real mutation first* (93 rows), then the restore,
  then compared all 5,120 cells, then rolled back. A restore drill that has nothing to restore
  proves nothing; this one had to actually undo something.
- **It recomputed every median independently in Python** from raw durations and got zero
  disagreements with the SQL across 93 assets — two methods, same numbers.

It also declined to widen into T-3 (1,477 `build_run_assets` rows) and T-4 (23 `build_runs`),
because D-12 part 5 admits `build_run_assets` for **telemetry aggregates only** and does not name
`build_runs` at all. Reported, not widened.

## One flagged discrepancy it deliberately did not close

`estimated_seconds` is **absent from the seed's `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET`** — good for this
repair's durability, but it means those 119 NULLs were **never inside the NULL-fill gate's column
coverage**, which touches D-33 part 2's second exemplar. **It read the code and did not run the
divergence report**, so it filed a discrepancy needing a query rather than a closed finding. That
distinction is the one I have been getting wrong, and it got it right.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
